※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1
※ pl4g-test: run=native,qemu exit=0
※ pl4g-test: diag=none

※ A variable of every width is written, so that each store the backends know is
※ exercised on every architecture.
※
※ The values stay within what one instruction can carry on every target: the
※ tightest is the twelve-bit signed immediate of one of them, so a larger
※ constant would need a sequence this compiler does not generate yet.
※ Only the first is read back; the point here is the store, so the other three
※ raise the warning about a variable nothing reads, on purpose.
let byte_value: mut u8 = 0u8

@[expect(4007)]
let half_value: mut u16 = 0u16

@[expect(4007)]
let word_value: mut u32 = 0u32

@[expect(4007)]
let wide_value: mut u64 = 0u64

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    byte_value ← 200u8
    half_value ← 1000u16
    word_value ← 2000u32
    wide_value ← 1500u64
    ※ what this used to say through the exit status, which is six bits now
    if byte_value ≠ 200u8:
        1u6
    else:
        0u6
